{#
	The placeholder reference, as markup, in one place (XIV-4, XIV-38).

	Two pages draw this list — the document templates page and the email templates
	one — and they draw it from the same `DocumentMarkers`, so drawing it from two
	copies of the same eight lines of Twig would have been the second thing to keep
	in step after the marker set itself.

	Only the *list* is shared. The prose around it is not, and deliberately so: one
	page is telling somebody what to type into Word and the other what to type into
	a textarea, and a sentence that covered both would be a sentence that helped
	neither.
#}
{% macro list(markers) %}
	<dl class="row small mb-0">
		{% for marker in markers %}
			<dt class="col-6 fw-normal">
				<code>{{ marker.token }}</code>
			</dt>
			<dd class="col-6 text-body-secondary">
				{{ marker.label }}

				{#
					A marker that draws a picture says so, and this one word is the
					whole answer to a question XIV-89 asked out loud (§5.7).

					A list offering `[tenant.logo]` next to `[tenant.name]` with
					nothing to tell them apart is a list that gets one of them
					pasted into the middle of a sentence — they are the same seven
					characters in the same brackets under the same heading — and
					what comes back is a picture wedged into a line of prose,
					which reads as the engine misbehaving rather than as the
					document saying what it was told to say. The label alone does
					not carry it: "This company's logo" is exactly what somebody
					would expect to substitute the *name* of a logo file.

					A badge rather than a sentence because it belongs to the row
					rather than to the section, and the reader is scanning tokens
					rather than reading paragraphs by the time they reach here.
				#}
				{% if marker.isImage %}
					<span class="badge text-bg-secondary-subtle text-secondary-emphasis fw-normal marker-image">
						<i class="bi bi-image" aria-hidden="true"></i>
						{{ 'document.marker_draws_a_picture'|trans }}
					</span>
				{% endif %}
			</dd>
		{% endfor %}
	</dl>
{% endmacro %}
